description:
Close substrate gap nonzero-field-treats-an-empty-array-as-nonzero-so-returns-empty-gaps-close-false.

SCOPE: this gap is about the VERIFIER's notion of a zero value. It is not about any detector, and not about whether any detector finds anything. Correcting the zero-test closes it completely.

In repos/development-vessel/src/resolvers/gap-to-feature.ts, inside verifyGapConditionAsync, "Defect heuristic 2" reads:

      if (nonzeroField !== null) {
        const val = inner[nonzeroField];
        if (val === 0 || val === null || val === undefined || val === '') {
          return 'present';
        }
        return 'absent';
      }

`nonzero_field` names a field that should be non-zero; a zero value means the gap's defect is still present. The list of zero-equivalents omits the empty array, so an empty array is treated as a healthy non-zero value and the function returns 'absent' — defect fixed — for any gap whose defect is an empty result.

Make exactly one edit. Use this short anchor verbatim; do not re-derive or paraphrase it.

Anchor:

val === 0 || val === null

It occurs once, on this line:

      if (val === 0 || val === null || val === undefined || val === '') {

Replace that entire line with:

      if (val === 0 || val === null || val === undefined || val === '' || (Array.isArray(val) && val.length === 0)) {

Nothing else changes. Do not modify the enclosing `if (nonzeroField !== null) {` block, its `return 'present';`, the following `return 'absent';`, or defect heuristics 1, 3 and 4 — those are out of scope and correct as they stand.

Acceptance: an empty array passed as the nonzero_field value returns 'present'. Every existing zero-equivalent (0, null, undefined, "") keeps its current 'present' verdict. Any non-empty array or non-zero scalar still returns 'absent' exactly as before, so nothing that passes today changes behaviour.

The file must typecheck. This vessel carries a standing baseline of pre-existing test failures unrelated to this change — judge on newly-failing tests, not absolute counts.

